and in the middle of a line of the six largest Thuringian cities, stretching from Eisenach in the west, via Gotha, Erfurt, Weimar and Jena, to Gera in the east. Together with Kassel and Göttingen, it is one of the cities with more than 100,000 inhabitants lying closest to the geographic centre of Germany. Erfurt is 100 km (62 mi) south-west of Leipzig, 250 km (155 mi) north-east of Frankfurt, 300 km (186 mi) south-west of Berlin and 400 km (249 mi) north of Munich.","image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Kr%C3%A4merbr%C3%BCcke%2C_Erfurt_6.jpg/330px-Kr%C3%A4merbr%C3%BCcke%2C_Erfurt_6.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Erfurt"},"twins":["Q134494","Q216","Q52842","Q1720","Q648","Q986627","Q44255","Q189328","Q41621","Q57719","Q771251","Q13329","Q2596788"],"climate":{"temp":[-0.4,0.6,4.1,9.1,13.6,17.6,19.9,19.5,14.9,9.6,4.7,0.9],"rain":[51,37,44,35,68,60,77,61,56,48,54,54],"cloud":[81,78,73,65,71,70,67,66,65,72,80,82]},"land":true,"transit":{"metroKm":0,"buswayKm":0,"bikeKm":50,"stations":0,"lines":0}}